ed to test port 5432 on node3 were killed after each use (cmd-cde6ea5c93794f53, cmd-73c1aeefc7964d95, cmd-f6cdb37a95e84187). However, one mutation was not reverted: while debugging non-interactive SSH to node1 as root, the executor added the cluster SSH public key to /root/.ssh/authorized_keys on the VyOS router (cmd-70e86e769e3949d4). This root-login attempt subsequently failed ('Permission denied', cmd-582545e82b5e43cb) and was abandoned in favor of the vyos user, but the authorized_keys entry itself was never removed, leaving a residual (unused but live) credential grant for root on the router.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-70e86e769e3949d4\",\"cmd-582545e82b5e43cb\",\"cmd-d1d03f026f894ca0\",\"cmd-52dcb10730fe4368\",\"cmd-186299b6cc5d4ed9\",\"cmd-c8ab8819cad5490d\",\"cmd-bcf5a67aebe04e08\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor built an Ansible playbook on the designated control node (node4) that configures node1 (VyOS) as a router with a forward-filter firewall permitting only TCP/5432 from lan-a to lan-b and rejecting all other lan-a<->lan-b traffic, and configures static addressing/routes on node2 and node3. It also discovered and remediated a host-level firewalld block on node3 that would otherwise have defeated the allowed flow. Functional evidence directly demonstrates the required allow/reject behavior via live TCP and ICMP probes, both before and after all four managed nodes were restarted one at a time with boot-id proofs, giving strong confidence the configuration is correctly implemented, Ansible-driven, and persists across reboots. The one notable gap is an unreverted, unsuccessful root SSH key addition on the VyOS node left over from troubleshooting.\",\"limitations\":[\"The provider-wide restart evidence protocol was followed for all four nodes with clear pre/post boot-id changes and functional re-verification, but this evaluation relies on the executor's self-selected command excerpts rather than exhaustive independent logs beyond what was provided.\",\"